/**
  @page examples-interface-traces Evaluating interface traces
  @brief Inspect two cell values, their jump and their arithmetic average.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-interface-traces-setting A discontinuous field

  A square split into two triangles has one interior facet. A piecewise
  constant field takes value one in region one and value three in region two.
  This small example makes side selection explicit before facet terms are
  used in a variational formulation.

  @snippet DG/InterfaceTraces.cpp interface-setup

  The mesh is the same two-triangle topology used in the
  @ref examples-geometry-connectivity "connectivity tutorial".

  @image html Geometry/Connectivity.png "Two cells meeting at one interior diagonal." width=720px

  @section examples-interface-traces-evaluation Select and combine traces

  The facet midpoint is mapped back into each incident cell. Evaluating the
  field on those two cell points obtains the one-sided values explicitly.
  `Jump` subtracts the two incident-cell values in the
  library's incidence order. `Average` forms their arithmetic mean.

  @snippet DG/InterfaceTraces.cpp interface-evaluate

  | Quantity | Expected value |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | Trace from region one | 1 |
  | Trace from region two | 3 |
  | Absolute jump | 2 |
  | Arithmetic average | 2 |

  The sign of a jump follows the incidence ordering. A flux formula must use
  the matching normal convention. Attribute-based trace selection gives an
  explicit side when modeling a material interface.

  @section examples-interface-traces-run Run and inspect

  Build the `InterfaceTraces` target. The program evaluates the midpoint of
  the interior facet, prints the four values and exports
  `InterfaceTraces.csv`. It checks the table above with tolerance
  @f$10^{-12}@f$ and returns `EXIT_FAILURE` if a check fails.

  The complete program is available in @ref DG/InterfaceTraces.cpp "InterfaceTraces.cpp".

  @section examples-interface-traces-next Continue

  - @ref guides-interfaces-dg "Facet forms and interior-penalty consistency"
  - @ref guides-boundary-conditions "Exterior boundary data"

  @example DG/InterfaceTraces.cpp
  @m_footernavigation
*/
